\begin_layout Abstract
Bioconductor is an open-source software project that provides tools for
 the analysis and comprehension of genomic data.
 One of the main advantages of Bioconductor is the availability of standard
 data representations and large number of analysis tools for genomic experiments.
 These tools allow researchers to efficiently store, manipulate, and analyze
 their data, leading to a deeper understanding of the underlying biological
 processes.
 However, Bioconductor data representations are currently limited to the
 R programming language.
 BiocPy is an effort to port these data representations in Python and integrate
 with existing scientific ecosystem e.g.
 Numpy, Scipy etc.
 This includes core data structures for managing genomic intervals (
\emph on
GenomicRanges
\emph default
), container classes for single (
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
) or multi-assay experiments (
\emph on
MultiAssayExperiment
\emph default
), packages to efficiently perform operations over matrices (
\emph on
mopsy
\emph default
), interfaces to parse and represent genomic experiments stored from R (
\emph on
rds2py
\emph default
) and others.
 We also extend 
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
 to support single-cell experiments (
\emph on
SingleCellExperiment
\emph default
, 
\emph on
SpatialExperiment
\emph default
).
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to Python's Package
 Index (PyPI).
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "http://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bioconductor
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "huber2015orchestrating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is an open-source community effort to develop software packages for the
 analysis of genomic data.
 It is widely used by researchers in the fields of genomics, bioinformatics,
 and computational biology to perform statistical analyses, visualize data,
 and develop predictive models.
 One of the key features of Bioconductor is its core data structure and
 representations to efficiently manage and represent various types of genomic
 experiments.
 However, these data representations are currently limited to the R programming
 language, which is a popular choice for statistical analysis but may not
 be familiar or accessible to researchers in other domains.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Python is a widely used programming language in the field of data science,
 application or interface development, and has a large and active community
 of users.
 Recent optimizations to the core of Python has made the language 10-60%
 faster for various workloads (
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.11.html#faster-cpython"

\end_inset

) .
 Python has a number of libraries and tools that are specifically designed
 for data analysis and has strong support for parallel computing, which
 could be useful for working with large datasets.
 The machine learning community has deeply invested in Python because of
 the availability of libraries like keras, pytorch to rapidly prototype,
 build, publish and deploy machine learning or artificial intelligence models.
 Researchers often spend more time in the data gathering part of model developme
nt because of the lack of an ecosystem of software packages to read, parse
 and represent genomic experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inspired by Bioconductor, several isolated efforts implemented genomic data
 representations in Python, e.g.
 the scverse ecosystem with AnnData and MuData representations, pyRanges
 for representing genomic intervals and genome annotations.
 However, these efforts are built for specific technologies (single cell
 in the context of scverse) and may not provide abstract classes to represent
 any type of genomic experiments.
 These projects also lack compatibility with each other and provide fragmented
 user experience across packages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy is an effort to bridge the gap and port core data structures and
 representations from Bioconductor in Python.
 tries to port familiar well established and robust core set of data representat
ions from Bioconductor to Python.
 This includes core data structures for managing genomic intervals and genome
 annotations (
\emph on
GenomicRanges
\emph default
), container classes for single (
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
) or multi-assay experiments (
\emph on
MultiAssayExperiment
\emph default
), packages to efficiently perform operations over matrices (
\emph on
mopsy
\emph default
), interfaces to parse and represent genomic experiments stored from R (
\emph on
rds2py
\emph default
) and many others (
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "http://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.) We also extend 
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
 to support specialized genomic experiments in single-cell (
\emph on
SingleCellExperiment
\emph default
, 
\emph on
SpatialExperiment
\emph default
).
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to Python's Package
 Index (PyPI).
 This effort to standardize representation of genomic experiments can foster
 coordinated efforts across the community to develop methods and tools for
 analysis and visualization of genomic data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Core Data Representations in BiocPy 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Genomic Ranges:
\series default
 One of the key data representations is the 
\emph on
GenomicRanges
\emph default
 package, which represents genomic intervals and annotations in a compact
 and efficient manner.
 This package is particularly useful for storing and manipulating large
 datasets, such as those obtained from high-throughput sequencing experiments.
 It allows researchers to easily select and subset specific intervals of
 interest, as well as to perform various types of analyses on the data,
 such as overlaps and intersections.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Summarized Experiments:
\series default
 Another important data representation is the 
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
 package, which stores and summarizes genomic data in a flexible and convenient
 manner.
 This package allows researchers to store multiple types of data, including
 raw data, processed data, and metadata, in a single object.
 It also provides tools for data manipulation and visualization, making
 it easier for researchers to explore and understand the experiment.
\end_layout

